    {
      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "I'm planning the data model for people and expenses with localStorage persistence, designing the split logic so remainders get distributed to the first people added, and sketching a greedy debt-settlement algorithm that minimizes the number of cash transfers between debtors and creditors.\n\nFor validation I'm deciding to accept amounts with optional dollar signs or commas, parsing to cents to avoid floating point errors, and requiring unique names, nonempty descriptions, valid payers, and at least one person selected in each split. I'm also planning to show expenses newest-first and to surface validation errors in a single dedicated error element per form while preserving the user's entered values on rejection.\n\nNew checkboxes for people default to checked, and I want to avoid wiping out user-unchecked states on re-render, so I'll only rebuild the split section incrementally when adding a person rather than fully re-rendering everything. I'm also setting up DOM construction with safe text insertion to avoid HTML injection, pulling in Fraunces and IBM Plex Sans fonts with sensible fallbacks, and sticking strictly to the defined color palette for borders, hover states, and error text rather than introducing new tints.\n\nI'm defining the money parsing logic that validates decimal input with up to two decimal places and converts to integer cents, plus persistence to localStorage under a versioned key holding people and expenses arrays. I'm working out the balance calculation: each expense adds to the payer's paid total and subtracts an even split (with remainder cents distributed by person order) across the selected participants, then using a greedy two-pointer algorithm between creditors and debtors to generate the minimal set of settle-up transfers.\n\nI'm laying out the empty-state messages and validation errors for each form (no people, no expenses, duplicate names, unselected split, zero amount), plus the DOM structure for the People, Expenses, Balances, and Settle Up sections with their forms, lists, and error containers. I'm also planning the rendering functions that rebuild split checkboxes and the payer select while preserving existing checked/selected state where possible, and finalizing the visual styling — wordmark mark, spacing, borders, tabular numbers for money, and hover effects using only allowed palette colors without opacity tricks. Now I'm ready to assemble all of this into the final HTML/CSS/JS file.\n\n",
      "signature": ""
